      "text": "sequence. If CCMS is used for PFAS removal, plug flow operating set - points are an important operational consideration, and further work to optimize the flush for PFAS clearance may be necessary to increase overall rejection. There are several possible explanations for the observed increase in rejection with recovery, as seen in for long-chain PFAS, particularly with NF ( Fig. 5 ). The formation of supramolecular PFAS structures has been suggested to increase rejection by increasing molecule size. While retentate concentrations were several orders of magnitude lower than the critical micelle concentration (CMC), aggregation of PFAAs has been observed with concentrations several orders of magnitudes lower than the CMC [ 81 ]. Supramolecular structure formation can also be mediated by divalent cations and humic acids [ 34 \u2013 38 ], which significantly in - crease in concentration within the recirculation loop throughout each sequence. Alternatively, increased rejection with increased recovery may also be related to PFAS interactions with the membrane. Previous studies have proposed PFOS dissolution into the PA layer [ 70 , 71 ] and the formation of a gel-like layer of PFOS at the surface of the membrane [ 44 ] as exclusion mechanisms to reduce the passage of PFOS, and Li et al. [ 39 ] reported that increased PFAA sorption to PA NF membranes was positively correlated with steady state rejection. Determining the"
